---
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ExternalLink from "@/components/ExternalLink.astro";
import ReportIssue from "@/components/ReportIssue.astro";
import { SITE_URL } from "@/config/site";
import { breadcrumbWithHome } from "@/lib/jsonld";
import { ROUTES, absoluteRoute } from "@/lib/routes";
const pageUrl = absoluteRoute(SITE_URL, ROUTES.methodology);
const methodologyJsonLd = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Article",
    headline: "Methodology",
    description:
      "How projectcert collects, normalizes, and verifies state EL teacher certification data — including the 2019 Leider et al. baseline and the 2026 per-state SEA re-verification workflow.",
    url: pageUrl,
    inLanguage: "en-US",
    publisher: { "@id": `${SITE_URL}/#organization` },
    isAccessibleForFree: true,
    citation: "https://doi.org/10.14507/epaa.29.5279",
  },
  breadcrumbWithHome([{ name: "Methodology", url: pageUrl }]),
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology"
  description="How projectcert collects, normalizes, and verifies state EL teacher certification data."
  type="article"
  jsonLd={methodologyJsonLd}
>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-8 prose-style">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-ink">Methodology</h1>

    <h2 class="mt-6 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Seed data</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-ink-muted">
      The 51-state baseline comes from Leider, Colombo &amp; Nerlino (2021), <em>Education Policy Analysis Archives</em> 29(100).
      The authors conducted a document analysis of every U.S. state education agency's (SEA) public credentialing documents
      between October and December 2019, plus each SEA's professional teaching standards.
      <ExternalLink class="text-accent hover:underline" href="https://doi.org/10.14507/epaa.29.5279"
        >Read the paper</ExternalLink
      >.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-6 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Use of AI in data collection and analysis</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-ink-muted">
      Beyond the 2019 baseline, the catalog has been re-verified, extended, and prepared for publication using an AI
      agent (Claude, Anthropic) under direct human supervision. The agent retrieved current SEA documents, extracted the
      relevant credentialing rules, drafted the per-state coding against the same coding scheme used for the 2019
      baseline, traced pre-2019 statutory and case-law history, and produced the prose surfaces that wrap the underlying
      data. Every field continues to trace to a cited source URL with a retrieval date, and dated source snapshots are
      archived per state in the project repository for independent inspection. The 2019 baseline coding from Leider,
      Colombo &amp; Nerlino (2021) was not generated by the agent; it is the authors' published document analysis,
      retained verbatim and used as the comparison point against which every subsequent re-verification is recorded.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-muted">
      AI-assisted extraction is fallible. The agent may misread an SEA regulation, conflate adjacent endorsements, miss
      a recent rule revision, or apply the wrong canonical category. Readers are asked to treat any field outside the
      original 2019 collection as provisional until cross-checked against the cited source URL, and to verify
      load-bearing claims directly with the SEA before relying on them. Corrections — including disagreements with the
      coding, missing history events, and broken or stale citations — are welcome via the form below.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-6 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Data captured</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-ink-muted">
      Each state record encodes, per the <a class="text-accent hover:underline" href={ROUTES.glossary}
        >canonical taxonomy</a
      >:
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-2 list-disc pl-6 text-ink-muted space-y-1">
      <li>The percentage of public-school students classified as ELs.</li>
      <li>
        Whether a bilingual education credential is offered, and (if so) whether it is available as a standalone
        certification, an add-on endorsement, or both.
      </li>
      <li>Whether an ELD credential is offered, and the same standalone/add-on breakdown.</li>
      <li>
        Requirements to earn each credential: completion of an approved program, coursework, practicum, content test,
        and (for bilingual) demonstrated proficiency in a non-English language.
      </li>
      <li>Whether SEI training is mandated for all teachers.</li>
      <li>
        Whether the SEA's professional teaching standards explicitly reference diverse / cultural / linguistic / EL
        students.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="mt-6 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Provenance</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-ink-muted">
      Every fact-bearing field on every state record cites at least one source — each with a URL, a retrieval date, and
      an attribution to either the Leider et al. 2021 collection or the 2026 re-verification pass. A state record with
      no cited source cannot be published; the build refuses it.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-6 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Two separate checks, and why the distinction matters</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-ink-muted">
      The 2019 baseline data is treated as a <em>seed</em>, not the published version. Turning a seed record into a
      published one takes two checks, performed by different people at different times. The catalog reports them
      separately because they answer different questions, and describes a state as <em>fully verified</em> only when both
      are complete.
    </p>

    <h3 class="mt-4 text-lg font-semibold text-ink">The source check — is the record current?</h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-ink-muted">
      A maintainer opens the State Education Agency's <em>current</em> public documents, re-reads the certification rules
      against the same coding scheme applied to the 2019 baseline, and re-codes the record from what those documents say today.
      Every divergence from the 2019 coding is written up in a dated note, and the documents consulted are archived alongside
      it so a later reader can see what was read and when. This answers whether the record reflects the agency's present requirements.
      It is a single pass by a single person, and it has been completed for all fifty states and the District of Columbia.
    </p>

    <h3 class="mt-4 text-lg font-semibold text-ink">Reviewer confirmation — has a second person checked it?</h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-ink-muted">
      A source check can be thorough and still carry a transcription error, a misread clause, or a citation pointing at
      the wrong page. So each displayed detail is confirmed <em>separately</em> by an authorized reviewer, signing in under
      their own identity, who opens the source cited for that specific detail and confirms it says what the record claims.
      Each confirmation records which detail, which reviewer, and which date. Where a reviewer disagrees, the correction is
      filed against that detail rather than applied silently.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2 text-ink-muted">
      A confirmation is not permanent. It is recorded against the value as it stood at the time, so if the value later
      changes, or a cited source stops resolving, the confirmation stops counting and the detail returns to the review
      queue. This is why a state's confirmed count can fall as well as rise.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2 text-ink-muted">
      Reviewer confirmation is deliberately the slower of the two, and it never promotes a record on its own — no
      automated process moves a state into the verified set.
      <a class="text-accent hover:underline" href={ROUTES.verification}>Track the per-state progress</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-6 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Reporting outdated or incorrect data</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-ink-muted">
      State credentialing requirements evolve regularly, and AI-assisted coding can introduce its own mistakes.
      Practitioners, SEA staff, and researchers who identify outdated, miscoded, or insufficiently cited entries are
      encouraged to flag them — field-level corrections from people who work with these credentials directly are
      preferred to reliance on the project's re-verification schedule alone.
    </p>

    <ReportIssue variant="inline" pageType="methodology" />
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
